              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Mandarin for size, then the Mandarin for a good fit."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: 上网",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "上网 — shàngwǎng — \"to go online\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "nǐ měitiān shàngwǎng ma — Do you go online every day?"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "The new character, in pieces: 网 (wǎng) — a frame open at the foot, like the outside of 两, with two small crosses inside. The next lesson puts it in your hand."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "上网 (shàngwǎng) is built from 上 'to go up, to get on' and wǎng 'net', both inherited from Middle Chinese, dzyangX and mjangX (Guangyun, in Baxter's transcription); the dictionary describes the net as knotted cord for hunting and fishing. Getting on to the net is a modern sense."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for everyday things."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
